Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/2] media: i2c: GalaxyCore GC2083 sensor driver

This adds a driver and a binding for the GalaxyCore GC2083, a 1920x1080
10-bit Bayer sensor with a two-lane CSI-2 output. It is the sensor on
Milk-V's CAM-GC2083 module, the camera sold for the Duo and Duo S.

It is an RFC because of one thing, stated up front: no frame has ever
come out of this driver. The Sophgo SG2000 on the Duo S has no mainline
CSI-2 receiver and no mainline ISP, so there is nothing downstream of the
sensor to receive pixels, and there is no other board in tree with this
sensor on it. Everything below was tested against real silicon over I2C,
and none of it involves a captured image.

What is known to work on hardware:

 - probe, including the chip ID read (0x2083)
 - runtime PM: the sensor genuinely loses power when suspended (register
   reads NAK) and answers again when resumed, with no clock reference
   leaked across repeated module load/unload and bind/unbind cycles
 - the control ranges, cross-checked arithmetically against the vendor's
   timings
 - s_stream(1), which writes the full init table and the stream-on
   sequence and returns success, with exposure and frame length reading
   back byte-exact afterwards

To get that far I wrote a throwaway platform driver that registers a
v4l2_device, waits for the sensor via the async notifier and calls
v4l2_device_register_subdev_nodes(), purely so that a /dev/v4l-subdev0
exists for v4l2-ctl to open. It is not part of this series and is not
proposed for upstream; it is mentioned only so it is clear how the above
was exercised.

What is therefore unverified, and what I would most like review on:

 - the Bayer order. SRGGB10 comes from the vendor's board files. Nothing
   here can confirm it.
 - whether the mirror and flip registers take effect. They are documented
   as shadowed and they read back as 0x00 on this part, so the writes
   cannot be observed on the bus and the result cannot be observed
   anywhere else either.
 - which parts of the vendor's init sequence are load-bearing. There is
   no public datasheet; the register table is reproduced as-is from
   GalaxyCore's reference settings carried in Sophgo's
   duo-buildroot-sdk-v2, and most of it is undocumented.

Two smaller notes:

 - The mode timings assume a 24MHz MCLK and are not recalculated from the
   actual rate. The Duo S clock tree cannot make exactly 24MHz - the
   nearest it reaches is 23.76MHz - so probe warns and carries on rather
   than failing, and the reported frame rate and link frequency are that
   0.99 out. Making the driver derive its timings from clk_get_rate()
   would be a bigger change than I wanted to make blind; I am happy to do
   it if that is preferred.
 - There is no DTS patch. A sensor node with nothing at the far end of
   its graph does not probe - fwnode_graph_get_endpoint_by_id() skips
   endpoints whose remote is unavailable - so an in-tree node has to wait
   for an SG2000 receiver driver to exist.

Based on v7.2-rc7. dt_binding_check and checkpatch --strict are clean,
and the driver builds warning-free at W=1 for riscv.

